Quantum $K$ theory for flag manifolds
\author{Givental, Shrair}
\abstract
The classical GKM formula has been extended to incorporate operations on $K$ theory for the complete flag manifold within a semisimple algebraic group. This extension enables the demonstration that certain operators preserve a specific class of functions under the application of a particular combinatorial structure known as the Berenstein-Gelfand graph. Consequently, it follows that the complete flag manifold's GKM presentation yields explicit descriptions of both the classical and quantum $B$-affine Grassmannian's $K$-representing functions, as well as their relationship to affine Grassmannian $K$-homology. Moreover, a new set of relations has been established for the quantum $B$-affine Grassmannian's $K$-representing functions, incorporating deformation parameters into the quantum Berezin relations for the positive half of the affine Grassmannian.

In the aerospace industry, ensuring efficient communication between airborne structures and attached components, such as satellite attachment mechanisms, is crucial. One effective way to enhance this engagement is through the use of adaptive structures capable of adjusting to irregularities in the interface, like compliant foams and viscoelastic materials however predicting their long-term effectiveness requires complex simulations over extended timeframes which can be highly resource-intensive. This study introduces a novel approach to optimize viscoelastic properties in strain-energy functions using surface-based measurements and an efficient algorithm combining level-set methods and adjoint techniques. Our results demonstrate the ability to achieve highly accurate solutions to this complex problem within a manageable number of iterations. Our proposed method is efficient and accurate, providing a potential solution to this complex problem.

